#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""sakura-map — a map of a codebase: the files that matter most first, and under each the signatures of its most-referenced definitions, with line numbers. No model.

  sakura-map [PATH]                  the map of the repository you're in (or of the directory PATH), within a character budget
  sakura-map --for "invoice total"   rank toward what a task mentions: file names, symbols or plain words
  sakura-map --focus FILE            rank toward a file you're working on (repeatable)
  sakura-map --budget N              at most N characters of map (default 4000; the driver's context is 32k)
  sakura-map --files                 only the ranked file list, with scores
  sakura-map --json                  every file and definition (name, kind, line, signature, rank) and which source read them
  sakura-map --tools                 what reads definitions on this machine (ast, the built-in patterns, universal-ctags)
  sakura-map --source builtin        don't use ctags even when it's installed
  sakura-map on [--budget N]         make the map standing context: hooks/repo_map.py puts it beside a message that looks like
                                     a code task in a git repo, ranked for that message, once a session unless the focus moves
  sakura-map off                     stop that
  sakura-map status                  on or off, and the budget
How: every source file's definitions (functions, classes, methods, types, routes) are read, Python exactly with `ast`, and
JavaScript/TypeScript, Vue and Svelte `<script>` blocks, PHP (with Laravel routes), Go, Rust, Ruby, Java, Kotlin, C/C++ and
shell with patterns; universal-ctags adds what the patterns miss when it's installed, never required. A file that mentions a name
defined in another file points at it; names that are common words, private, or defined in many files count for little.
PageRank over that graph ranks the files, and each definition by the rank that flows to it. `--for`, `--focus` and files
changed in the working tree pull the ranking toward them; files the task names come first.
In a git repo it reads what `git ls-files` lists, so .gitignore is respected; vendored, minified, generated and huge files
are skipped, and at most 6000 files are read (SAKURA_MAP_MAX_FILES). What each file defines is cached under
~/.cache/sakura/map/ by path, mtime and size, so the second run is quick. Off by default as standing context: it changes
what the model reads, so turn it on after `evals/run.py` passes with it on your driver.
"""
import ast
import collections
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time

